\details{
\code{compare_fits()} is the general name of this step and \code{compare_allocations()} its
earlier name: the two are the same function, and they differ only in the name that a
message gives for the step.

Loss differences from the reference are reported only when the comparison is
like for like: the same observed targets, loss function and arguments
(Poisson fits: the same floor, a fit that stored none used 1e-9, or floors
that change neither loss), demand, supply, origin and facility IDs and
prepared travel times. Travel times are checked only when \code{models} are
supplied, and each model must reproduce its own fit; otherwise loss
differences are left missing. Fits of models with demand groups
(\code{\link[=prepare_allocation]{prepare_allocation()}}) are comparable only with each other, with the same
group IDs and, once \code{models} are supplied, the same demand and the same
travel times for every group; a shared-parameter fit and a \code{by_group} fit on
the same group-labelled target answer whether the group decays differ. The
table then shows the free parameters, such as \verb{sigma.<group>}, and coverage
comes from the pooled outputs. Contact and capacity values remain comparable
across specifications, but they are conditional on each specification's
inputs.

For fits to the outside-count outputs (\code{flow_outside}, \code{utilization_outside},
\code{utilization_outside_by_group}), the \code{count_correlation} is over the whole
target, the \code{"(outside)"} cells included, so it is not comparable with the
correlation of a fit to facility counts without outside counts. The
capacity-only comparator of \code{\link[=check_allocation]{check_allocation()}} applies to facility totals
without outside counts only.

With \code{zones}, each fit is also summarized by zone using contact-weighted
aggregation, and the zones whose reporting class differs from the reference
are listed. Classes require an adequacy cut in \code{coverage_args}; each fit uses
its own regional contact as the contact cut unless \code{contact_cut} is given.
}
\examples{
# West Cornwall: the small areas and practices west of easting 170 km
areas <- sf::st_drop_geometry(cornwall_lsoa)
areas <- areas[!areas$scilly & areas$cent_x < 170000, ]
gp <- sf::st_drop_geometry(cornwall_gp)
gp <- gp[!gp$scilly & gp$easting < 170000, ]
d <- prep_data(stats::setNames(areas$registered, areas$lsoa),
               stats::setNames(gp$gp_fte_extg, gp$practice),
               cornwall_travel[areas$lsoa, gp$practice])

# Registrations of these residents at these practices
flows <- cornwall_flows[cornwall_flows$lsoa \%in\% areas$lsoa &
                          cornwall_flows$practice \%in\% gp$practice, ]
totals <- obs_totals(tapply(flows$patients, flows$practice, sum))

# The same counts fitted with two decay kernels
gaussian <- spax(d, spec_clm(decay_gaussian()), observed = totals)
exponential <- spax(d, spec_clm(decay_exponential()), observed = totals)

# Loss differences are measured from the first fit
comparison <- compare_fits(list(gaussian = gaussian, exponential = exponential))
comparison
as.data.frame(comparison)[, c("specification", "parameters", "loss_difference")]
}
